WEBVTT

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.430
Hour zero and hour twenty
three are neighbors on a clock.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:07.650
A numeric hour column
has a jump at midnight,

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:12.510
while one hot indicators give no
built in notion of neighboring hours.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:17.990
A sine and cosine pair places those
hours close together on a circle.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.630
Hour eight occupies its own
position farther around the clock.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.100
The highlighted short connection shows why
twenty three and zero are nearby in this

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:28.830
representation.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.650
That is a useful input geometry,

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.920
but the prediction function still depends
on what the learner can do with those

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:36.530
coordinates.

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:41.587
Divide the hour by its period,
twenty four,

00:00:41.587 --> 00:00:45.097
and multiply by two pi to get an angle.

00:00:45.587 --> 00:00:47.997
Apply both sine and cosine.

00:00:48.187 --> 00:00:49.417
At hour eight,

00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:54.717
the values are about zero point eight
six six and minus zero point five.

00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:57.677
At zero the pair is zero and one.

00:00:58.187 --> 00:01:03.471
At twenty three it is about minus zero
point two five nine and zero point nine

00:01:03.471 --> 00:01:04.297
six six,

00:01:04.727 --> 00:01:06.317
close to the midnight point.

00:01:06.927 --> 00:01:09.097
Weekday and month use their own periods.

00:01:09.727 --> 00:01:13.297
January is indexed at zero in
our declared transformation.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:21.130
The source recovers calendar indices from
the frozen one hot vector and computes

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:21.860
each pair.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:23.960
It reads no outcomes.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.440
Three pairs make six columns;

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.200
holiday and working day add two,

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:30.820
giving eight inputs.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:36.420
We retain their exact order and fit
any standardization on training only.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.580
The pair still distinguishes
the discrete hour positions.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:44.680
What changes is how a linear
function can combine them.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:50.170
This representation is compared with the
original one hot model using the same

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:54.140
training solve,
no penalty and the same later rows.

00:01:56.317 --> 00:02:01.957
A linear model can multiply the sine and
cosine by one coefficient each and add

00:02:01.957 --> 00:02:02.687
an intercept.

00:02:03.377 --> 00:02:08.567
That combination produces one sinusoidal
wave for the hourly contribution.

00:02:09.217 --> 00:02:13.737
It cannot freely assign twenty four
independent hour effects as one hot

00:02:13.737 --> 00:02:14.667
columns can.

00:02:15.157 --> 00:02:20.107
Two sharp commuting peaks are therefore
difficult to express with this one pair.

00:02:20.817 --> 00:02:25.527
The calendar position remains encoded,
but linear expressive freedom has changed.

00:02:26.497 --> 00:02:30.597
Adding harmonics or using another
nonlinear learner could change that

00:02:30.597 --> 00:02:31.167
limitation;

00:02:31.657 --> 00:02:33.967
either would be a new declared comparison,

00:02:34.397 --> 00:02:36.607
not something already implemented here.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:42.290
The cyclic training half squared
error is about four thousand,

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:46.783
eight hundred and ten, higher than the
one hot reference near three thousand,

00:02:46.783 --> 00:02:48.100
one hundred and sixty eight.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:53.540
Raw validation absolute error is
slightly lower for cyclic features,

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.420
but after consistent clipping it is worse.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.480
Root mean squared error is also worse.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.800
These are stable training solves,

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:06.480
so unfinished gradient optimization does
not explain the structural difference.

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:11.540
A smaller representation has not supplied
a universally stronger predictor.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:13.240
Keep the representation,

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:17.000
output policy and metric
beside each result.

00:03:19.108 --> 00:03:22.098
At the later Monday eight oclock hour,

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:26.715
the cyclic model predicts only about
fifty two rentals against four hundred and

00:03:26.715 --> 00:03:27.158
twenty.

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:31.018
The one hot reference predicts
about one hundred and seventy five.

00:03:32.608 --> 00:03:34.418
The clock picture is sensible,

00:03:34.748 --> 00:03:38.418
but the resulting linear function
is too restrictive for this example.

00:03:39.128 --> 00:03:43.298
That does not make circular encoding
useless for every learner or problem.

00:03:43.728 --> 00:03:47.808
It tells us what this particular linear
pair can express under the frozen

00:03:47.808 --> 00:03:48.478
contract.

00:03:49.008 --> 00:03:53.178
We next add an explicit hour
by working day interaction,

00:03:53.568 --> 00:03:55.878
which answers a different
structural question.

